        <ARI_PrevalenceDesc>Worldwide incidence: 1.0 cases per 666,667. US incidence: 1.0 cases per 666,667.</ARI_PrevalenceDesc>
        <ARI_Pubmed>https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/17047148/</ARI_Pubmed>
        <ARI_RefLink>Hemophilia Federation of America | https://www.hemophiliafed.org</ARI_RefLink>
        <ARI_RefLink>Link to Healthline | https://www.healthline.com/health/hemophilia-a/acquired-hemophilia</ARI_RefLink>
        <ARI_RefLink>National Bleeding Disorders Foundation | https://www.bleeding.org</ARI_RefLink>
        <ARI_SNOMED>785308008</ARI_SNOMED>
        <ARI_SurveyCode>AH</ARI_SurveyCode>
        <ARI_Synonym>Acquired haemophilia</ARI_Synonym>
        <ARI_UMLS>C1096116</ARI_UMLS>
        <ARI_Version>2</ARI_Version>
        <rdfs:comment>Acquired Hemophilia is an autoimmune disorder in which the immune system targets and damages a type of protein in the blood necessary for clotting.  The result is spontaneous bleeding that is difficult to stop, which may occur both on the surface of the skin and internal to the body, affecting muscles and organs.  Patients also bruise easily with little trauma.  A range of triggers can cause severe bleeding episodes, including physical activity, surgery, and giving birth.  Bleeding may also occur with no obvious cause.  Treatments are available to control bleeding episodes.  Without medical care, patients are at risk of muscle death or of life-threatening bleeding complications.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>Acquired hemophilia</rdfs:label>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


