        <ARI_ClinicalSubtype>Hyperacute/Fulminant ADEM - Severe, rapidly progressing form.</ARI_ClinicalSubtype>
        <ARI_ClinicalSubtype>Monophasic ADEM - Single episode, most common form.</ARI_ClinicalSubtype>
        <ARI_ClinicalSubtype>Multiphasic ADEM (MDEM) - Second episode with new lesions.</ARI_ClinicalSubtype>
        <ARI_ClinicalSubtype>Recurrent ADEM - Multiple episodes without new triggers.</ARI_ClinicalSubtype>
        <ARI_DOID>639</ARI_DOID>
        <ARI_DXCODE>83942000</ARI_DXCODE>
        <ARI_DefSource>Filippi and Rocca 2020; https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-030-38621-4_5; Tenembaum 2002; https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/12391351/</ARI_DefSource>
        <ARI_ICD10>G04.0</ARI_ICD10>
        <ARI_ID>ARI:0001003</ARI_ID>
        <ARI_MESH>D004673</ARI_MESH>
        <ARI_MONDO>0019383</ARI_MONDO>
        <ARI_NCI>C34578</ARI_NCI>
        <ARI_OMOP>374021</ARI_OMOP>
        <ARI_ORPHANET>83597</ARI_ORPHANET>
        <ARI_Obsolete>false</ARI_Obsolete>
        <ARI_PrevalenceDesc>Worldwide incidence: 1.0 cases per 125,000 to 250,000. US incidence: 1.0 cases per 8,333,333 to 100,000,000; 1.0 cases per 166,667 to 1,428,571.</ARI_PrevalenceDesc>
        <ARI_Pubmed>https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/12391351/</ARI_Pubmed>
        <ARI_RefLink>Acute disseminated encephalomyelitis support group | http://www.acutedisseminatedencephalomyelitis.org/</ARI_RefLink>
        <ARI_RefLink>Link to Cleveland Clinic | https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/14266-acute-disseminated-encephalomyelitis-adem</ARI_RefLink>
        <ARI_RefLink>Link to Healthline | https://www.healthline.com/health/acute-disseminated-encephalomyelitis-vs-ms</ARI_RefLink>
        <ARI_RefLink>My Life With ADEM/ In Their Own Words | https://wearesrna.org/my-life-with-adem/</ARI_RefLink>
        <ARI_RefLink>Siegel Rare Neuroimmune Association | https://wearesrna.org/</ARI_RefLink>
        <ARI_RefLink>Transverse Myelitis Association | https://wearesrna.org/</ARI_RefLink>
        <ARI_SNOMED>83942000</ARI_SNOMED>
        <ARI_SurveyCode>ADEM</ARI_SurveyCode>
        <ARI_Synonym>ADEM</ARI_Synonym>
        <ARI_SynonymWithdrawn>Hurst&apos;s disease | subtype | eponym for acute haemorrhagic leukoencephalitis, the hyperacute haemorrhagic variant of ADEM (subtype &quot;Hyperacute/Fulminant ADEM&quot;)</ARI_SynonymWithdrawn>
        <ARI_SynonymWithdrawn>Nonvasculitic autoimmune inflammatory meningoencephalitis | distinct | NAIM/SREAT-spectrum steroid-responsive encephalopathy, a different entity from ADEM</ARI_SynonymWithdrawn>
        <ARI_SynonymWithdrawn>Weston-Hurst syndrome | subtype | eponym for acute haemorrhagic leukoencephalitis, the hyperacute haemorrhagic variant of ADEM (subtype &quot;Hyperacute/Fulminant ADEM&quot;)</ARI_SynonymWithdrawn>
        <ARI_UMLS>C0014059</ARI_UMLS>
        <ARI_Version>2</ARI_Version>
        <rdfs:comment>Acute disseminated encephalomyelitis (ADEM) is caused when inflammation stemming from fever or immunization damages the myelin sheaths of nerves in the brain, spinal cord, and occasionally the optic nerves, leading to abnormal nerve function.  Onset is brief but intense and occurs predominantly in children.  Patients often experience complete recovery within 6 months and do not experience relapses. The risk of developing ADEM depends on several factors, including genetics, exposure to infectious organisms, immunization exposure, and lighter skin pigmentation. ADEM can affect people of all ethnic backgrounds globally. Between 50% to 85% of ADEM cases are linked to a prior infection or vaccination, with many following a viral or bacterial infection. Nonetheless, the specific pathogen causing ADEM is often unknown.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>Acute disseminated encephalomyelitis</rdfs:label>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


