        <ARI_MONDO>0100480</ARI_MONDO>
        <ARI_NCI>C26689</ARI_NCI>
        <ARI_OMOP>443394</ARI_OMOP>
        <ARI_ORPHANET>85138</ARI_ORPHANET>
        <ARI_Obsolete>false</ARI_Obsolete>
        <ARI_PrevalenceDesc>Worldwide prevalence: 1.0 cases per 7,143 to 11,111. US prevalence: 1.0 cases per 4,525 to 23,981.</ARI_PrevalenceDesc>
        <ARI_Pubmed>https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/26437215/</ARI_Pubmed>
        <ARI_RefLink>Addison&apos;s support advocacy blog | http://addisonssupport.blogspot.com/</ARI_RefLink>
        <ARI_RefLink>Clearly alive | http://clearlyalive.blogspot.com/</ARI_RefLink>
        <ARI_RefLink>Link to Cleveland Clinic | https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/15095-addisons-disease</ARI_RefLink>
        <ARI_RefLink>Link to Healthline | https://www.healthline.com/health/addisons-disease</ARI_RefLink>
        <ARI_RefLink>Link to Mayo Clinic | https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/addisons-disease/symptoms-causes/syc-20350293</ARI_RefLink>
        <ARI_RefLink>Living with an Addisonian | https://livingwithaddisons.wordpress.com/</ARI_RefLink>
        <ARI_RefLink>My name is Kimberly and I have Addison&apos;s | http://kimberlyraeauthor.blogspot.com/2012/10/my-name-is-kimberly-and-i-have-addisons.html</ARI_RefLink>
        <ARI_RefLink>National Adrenal Disease Foundation | http://www.nadf.us/</ARI_RefLink>
        <ARI_SNOMED>363732003</ARI_SNOMED>
        <ARI_SurveyCode>AD</ARI_SurveyCode>
        <ARI_Synonym>Autoimmune adrenalitis</ARI_Synonym>
        <ARI_Synonym>Primary adrenal insufficiency</ARI_Synonym>
        <ARI_SynonymWithdrawn>Adrenal Insufficiency | broader | umbrella term including secondary/tertiary and non-autoimmune adrenal insufficiency</ARI_SynonymWithdrawn>
        <ARI_SynonymWithdrawn>Adrenal cortical hypofunction | broader | parent class of Addison&apos;s disease in the Disease Ontology; covers non-autoimmune causes</ARI_SynonymWithdrawn>
        <ARI_UMLS>C0001403</ARI_UMLS>
        <ARI_Version>2</ARI_Version>
        <rdfs:comment>Addison&apos;s disease is a hormonal disorder that occurs when the adrenal glands do not produce enough of certain hormones. The two hormones that tend to be affected the most are cortisol and aldosterone. Symptoms may occur slowly over several months, and most people do not realize these are symptoms of Addison’s and ignore them. Early symptoms include fatigue, dizziness or fainting due to low blood pressure, sweating due to low blood sugar, upset stomach, abdominal pain, and muscle cramps or weakness. If symptoms become worse quickly, then it can develop into an adrenal crisis, resulting in death if not treated quickly enough.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>Addison&apos;s disease</rdfs:label>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


