        <ARI_ClinicalSubtype>Autoimmune Hemolytic Anemia, Cold (Cold Agglutinin Disease) - Mediated by IgM antibodies at cold temperatures.</ARI_ClinicalSubtype>
        <ARI_ClinicalSubtype>Autoimmune Hemolytic Anemia, Warm - Caused by IgG antibodies active at body temperature.</ARI_ClinicalSubtype>
        <ARI_DOID>718</ARI_DOID>
        <ARI_DXCODE>413603009</ARI_DXCODE>
        <ARI_DefSource>Bozzi 2023; https://ashpublications.org/blood/article/142/Supplement%201/5202/501456/Prevalence-and-Incidence-of-Primary-Autoimmune</ARI_DefSource>
        <ARI_ICD10>D59.1</ARI_ICD10>
        <ARI_ID>ARI:0001032</ARI_ID>
        <ARI_MESH>D000744</ARI_MESH>
        <ARI_MONDO>0020108</ARI_MONDO>
        <ARI_NCI>C34378</ARI_NCI>
        <ARI_OMOP>441269</ARI_OMOP>
        <ARI_ORPHANET>98375</ARI_ORPHANET>
        <ARI_Obsolete>false</ARI_Obsolete>
        <ARI_PrevalenceDesc>Worldwide prevalence: 1.0 cases per 5,917 to 10,000. US prevalence: 1.0 cases per 5,917 to 10,000. Note on the US figure: Searched afterwards. Source: Bozzi et al. (PLoS One 2025) / Roumier et al., US AIHA prevalence.</ARI_PrevalenceDesc>
        <ARI_RefLink>Link to Cleveland Clinic | https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/22349-autoimmune-hemolytic-anemia</ARI_RefLink>
        <ARI_RefLink>Link to Healthline | https://www.healthline.com/health/idiopathic-autoimmune-hemolytic-anemia</ARI_RefLink>
        <ARI_SNOMED>413603009</ARI_SNOMED>
        <ARI_SurveyCode>AHA</ARI_SurveyCode>
        <ARI_UMLS>C0002880</ARI_UMLS>
        <ARI_Version>2</ARI_Version>
        <rdfs:comment>In autoimmune hemolytic anemia, red blood cells are destroyed by antibodies. In some cases, the antibodies are active only when the person is warm, in which case it is called warm autoimmune hemolytic anemia. In other cases, the antibodies are active when the person is cold, and it is called cold agglutinin disease, and in others, the antibodies can be active regardless of the person&apos;s body temperature. There is also a form of hemolytic anemia called paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria, in which a genetic mutation causes antibodies to cause the same kind of destruction of red blood cells.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>Autoimmune hemolytic anemia</rdfs:label>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


