        <ARI_PrevalenceDesc>Worldwide prevalence: 1.0 cases per 50,000 to 100,000. US prevalence: 1.0 cases per 50,000 to 100,000. Note on the US figure: Searched afterwards. Source: Clarke et al. / NORD, isolated idiopathic/autoimmune hypoparathyroidism.</ARI_PrevalenceDesc>
        <ARI_SNOMED>75316000</ARI_SNOMED>
        <ARI_UMLS>C0271865</ARI_UMLS>
        <ARI_Version>2.1</ARI_Version>
        <rdfs:comment>Autoimmune hypoparathyroidism is an endocrine condition where autoantibodies, often targeting the calcium-sensing receptor or NALP5, lead to the immune destruction of the parathyroid glands. Insufficient production of parathyroid hormone results in hypocalcemia, muscle cramping, tetany, and numbness, frequently occurring as a component of autoimmune polyendocrine syndrome type 1. Management requires active vitamin D analogs and calcium supplementation.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>Autoimmune hypoparathyroidism</rdfs:label>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


