        <ARI_ClinicalSubtype>Autoimmune Oophoritis, Associated with APS - Occurs with autoimmune polyendocrine syndrome.</ARI_ClinicalSubtype>
        <ARI_ClinicalSubtype>Autoimmune Oophoritis, Isolated - Targets ovaries, causing infertility.</ARI_ClinicalSubtype>
        <ARI_DefSource>Rudnicka 2018 and Tsigkou 2008; https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/18211971/</ARI_DefSource>
        <ARI_ID>ARI:0001041</ARI_ID>
        <ARI_MESH>C538274</ARI_MESH>
        <ARI_MONDO>0021950</ARI_MONDO>
        <ARI_Obsolete>false</ARI_Obsolete>
        <ARI_PrevalenceDesc>Worldwide prevalence: 1.0 cases per 5,000 to 10,000. US prevalence: 1.0 cases per 5,000; 4% of the 1% of women with primary ovarian insufficiency - prevalence is therefore 4% of 1% of 50% of US population.</ARI_PrevalenceDesc>
        <ARI_Pubmed>https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/18211971/</ARI_Pubmed>
        <ARI_RefLink>Link to Healthline | https://www.healthline.com/health/womens-health/oophoritis</ARI_RefLink>
        <ARI_SurveyCode>AOO</ARI_SurveyCode>
        <ARI_Synonym>Premature ovarian failure due to autoimmune oophoritis</ARI_Synonym>
        <ARI_UMLS>C0878654</ARI_UMLS>
        <ARI_Version>2</ARI_Version>
        <rdfs:comment>In autoimmune oophoritis, the immune system mistakenly attacks the ovaries, causing inflammation, atrophy, and fibrosis. These changes keep the ovaries from working normally, and the result can be primary ovarian insufficiency (POI). Autoimmune oophoritis is associated with lupus, pernicious anemia, myasthenia gravis, autoimmune polyendocrine syndrome type I or type II, and other autoimmune conditions. Special blood tests and imaging are used to diagnose this condition.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>Autoimmune oophoritis</rdfs:label>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


