        <ARI_PrevalenceDesc>Worldwide incidence: 1.0 cases per 100,000 to 200,000. US incidence: 1.0 cases per 100,000 to 200,000. Note on the US figure: Searched afterwards. Source: Petzold et al. / Lancet Neurology, isolated autoimmune optic neuropathy.</ARI_PrevalenceDesc>
        <ARI_SNOMED>838324008</ARI_SNOMED>
        <ARI_UMLS>C5681239</ARI_UMLS>
        <ARI_Version>2.1</ARI_Version>
        <rdfs:comment>Autoimmune optic neuritis is an immune-mediated inflammation of the optic nerve that occurs independently of multiple sclerosis or neuromyelitis optica spectrum disorders. Patients suffer acute, painful loss of vision that may recur or become chronic (CRION). Rapid administration of high-dose corticosteroids usually improves visual acuity, while long-term immunosuppression helps prevent irreversible optic nerve atrophy.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>Autoimmune optic neuropathy</rdfs:label>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


