        <ARI_ChangeLog>2026-10-15T12:40:37+00:00 | Leon Islas | Imported from ARI Master List 2026-10-08 from Leon Islas, ALPHABETICAL row 59: definition, disease type Unconfirmed, 4 associated diseases (4 linked to ARI diseases), demographic bias, 5 pathway steps, 4 symptoms.</ARI_ChangeLog>
        <ARI_ChangeLog>2026-10-15T15:44:20+00:00 | KrishnaTO | Symptom review (ARI#132): removed 1 non-symptom entry from hasSymptom and retired it (ARI_Obsolete true): &apos;Retinal perivascular cuffing, sheathing, and vascular leakage on angiography&apos;. Added to description: &quot;Angiography shows retinal perivascular cuffing, sheathing and vascular leakage in 90–100% of patients.&quot;</ARI_ChangeLog>
        <ARI_ChangeLog>2026-10-15T16:44:19+00:00 | migration (#229) | Moved to prevalence &amp; incidence figures: Prevalence US 0.75/100k; Prevalence Worldwide 0.75/100k</ARI_ChangeLog>
        <ARI_ChangeLog>2026-10-16T18:30:39+00:00 | KrishnaTO | Clinical biomarkers: added &apos;Retinal angiography&apos; (Imaging, sensitivity 90–100%) from the retired symptom Sym_0001046_1: &apos;Retinal perivascular cuffing, sheathing, and vascular leakage on angiography&apos;</ARI_ChangeLog>
        <ARI_ID>ARI:0001046</ARI_ID>
        <ARI_IsGrouping>true</ARI_IsGrouping>
        <ARI_Obsolete>false</ARI_Obsolete>
        <ARI_PrevalenceDesc>Worldwide prevalence: 1.0 cases per 100,000 to 200,000. US prevalence: 1.0 cases per 100,000 to 200,000. Note on the US figure: Searched afterwards. Source: Rosenbaum et al. / American Uveitis Society, primary retinal vasculitis.</ARI_PrevalenceDesc>
        <ARI_Version>2.1</ARI_Version>
        <rdfs:comment>Autoimmune retinal vasculitis is an inflammatory condition affecting the blood vessels of the retina that arises without an associated systemic rheumatic disease. Inflammation and occlusion of retinal vessels can cause blurred vision, blind spots, floaters, and potential retinal ischemia. Treatment involves localized and systemic corticosteroids and immunomodulatory agents to preserve eyesight. Angiography shows retinal perivascular cuffing, sheathing and vascular leakage in 90–100% of patients.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>Autoimmune retinal vasculitis</rdfs:label>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


