        <ARI_RefLink>Thyroid Federation International | https://www.thyroid-fed.org/</ARI_RefLink>
        <ARI_RefLink>Thyroid Nation | https://thyroidnation.com/</ARI_RefLink>
        <ARI_SNOMED>66944004</ARI_SNOMED>
        <ARI_SurveyCode>AT</ARI_SurveyCode>
        <ARI_SynonymWithdrawn>Chronic lymphocytic thyroiditis | subtype | names Hashimoto&apos;s thyroiditis (MONDO:0007699), now its own subtype entry ARI:0001216</ARI_SynonymWithdrawn>
        <ARI_SynonymWithdrawn>Hashimoto thyroiditis | subtype | names Hashimoto&apos;s thyroiditis (MONDO:0007699), now its own subtype entry ARI:0001216</ARI_SynonymWithdrawn>
        <ARI_SynonymWithdrawn>Hashimoto&apos;s disease | subtype | names Hashimoto&apos;s thyroiditis (MONDO:0007699), now its own subtype entry ARI:0001216</ARI_SynonymWithdrawn>
        <ARI_SynonymWithdrawn>Hashimoto&apos;s thyroiditis | subtype | names Hashimoto&apos;s thyroiditis (MONDO:0007699), now its own subtype entry ARI:0001216</ARI_SynonymWithdrawn>
        <ARI_UMLS>C0920350</ARI_UMLS>
        <ARI_Version>2</ARI_Version>
        <rdfs:comment>Autoimmune thyroiditis is a chronic disorder where the immune system creates antibodies that attack the thyroid and cause inflammation. This inflammation may initially cause excess release of thyroid hormones (hyperthyroidism ) but over time, this damage leads to insufficient thyroid hormone production (hypothyroidism), causing fatigue, weight gain, and depression. Genetic and environmental factors may play a role in the development of this disorder. There is no cure, but hormone treatments are available to manage symptoms.  

Several names are used for autoimmune thyroiditis. When it is known to be autoimmune, it is called Hashimoto&apos;s when the thyroid gland is enlarged (called a goiter), and (less commonly) Ord&apos;s thyroiditis, when the thyroid gland shrinks.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>Autoimmune thyroiditis</rdfs:label>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


