        <ARI_ClinicalSubtype>Autoimmune Urticaria/Angioedema, Complement-Mediated - Linked to complement system dysregulation.</ARI_ClinicalSubtype>
        <ARI_ClinicalSubtype>Autoimmune Urticaria/Angioedema, Mast Cell-Mediated - Caused by abnormal immune response.</ARI_ClinicalSubtype>
        <ARI_DXCODE>402402005</ARI_DXCODE>
        <ARI_DefSource>Murdaca 2023; https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC9953398/</ARI_DefSource>
        <ARI_ID>ARI:0001050</ARI_ID>
        <ARI_OMOP>4221673</ARI_OMOP>
        <ARI_Obsolete>false</ARI_Obsolete>
        <ARI_PrevalenceDesc>Worldwide prevalence: 1.0 cases per 100 to 200. US prevalence: 1.0 cases per 125.</ARI_PrevalenceDesc>
        <ARI_RefLink>Link to Cleveland Clinic | https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/22900-chronic-hives-chronic-idiopathic-urticaria</ARI_RefLink>
        <ARI_RefLink>Link to Healthline | https://www.healthline.com/health/skin/autoimmune-hives</ARI_RefLink>
        <ARI_SNOMED>402402005</ARI_SNOMED>
        <ARI_SurveyCode>NULL</ARI_SurveyCode>
        <ARI_UMLS>C1304196</ARI_UMLS>
        <ARI_Version>2</ARI_Version>
        <rdfs:comment>Both autoimmune urticaria and autoimmune angioedema are skin conditions associated with other autoimmune diseases. Urticaria is marked by raised, itchy, and red or pale areas, known as hives. Angioedema is swelling in skin and mucous membranes, including the mouth and throat, and is a more serious condition than urticaria. 

While they are also diseases on their own, patients will receive a diagnosis of a chronic version of the disease because the ICD coding system used in the medical record does not have codes for either disease. The SNOMED coding system (used in Europe) has a code for each condition, and for this combined term.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>Autoimmune urticaria and/or angioedema</rdfs:label>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


