        <ARI_MONDO>0030703</ARI_MONDO>
        <ARI_OMOP>4146124</ARI_OMOP>
        <ARI_Obsolete>false</ARI_Obsolete>
        <ARI_PrevalenceDesc>Worldwide prevalence: 1.0 cases per 2,000 to 5,000. US prevalence: 1.0 cases per 2,000 to 5,000. Note on the US figure: Searched afterwards. Source: Watts et al. / ACR, aggregated systemic vasculitides prevalence.</ARI_PrevalenceDesc>
        <ARI_RefLink>Kelly&apos;s Vasculitis Blog | http://kellysblogri.blogspot.com/</ARI_RefLink>
        <ARI_RefLink>Link to Cleveland Clinic | https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/12101-vasculitis</ARI_RefLink>
        <ARI_RefLink>Link to Healthline | https://www.healthline.com/health/autoimmune-vasculitis</ARI_RefLink>
        <ARI_RefLink>The Churg Strauss Syndrome Association | http://www.cssassociation.org/</ARI_RefLink>
        <ARI_RefLink>Vasculitis Foundation | http://www.vasculitisfoundation.org/</ARI_RefLink>
        <ARI_SNOMED>427213005</ARI_SNOMED>
        <ARI_SurveyCode>AV</ARI_SurveyCode>
        <ARI_UMLS>C1328843</ARI_UMLS>
        <ARI_Version>2</ARI_Version>
        <rdfs:comment>Autoimmune vasculitis is a broad category of autoimmune diseases that all involve inflammation of the walls of blood vessels.  A more specific form, inflammation of arteries, is called arteritis.  It causes changes in the walls of blood vessels, including thickening, weakening, narrowing and scarring. These changes restrict blood flow, resulting in organ and tissue damage.

There are many types of vasculitis, and most of them are rare. Vasculitis might affect just one organ, such as your skin, or it may involve several. The condition can be short term (acute) or long lasting (chronic).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>Autoimmune vasculitis</rdfs:label>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


