        <ARI_ID>ARI:0001059</ARI_ID>
        <ARI_OMOP>3654620</ARI_OMOP>
        <ARI_Obsolete>false</ARI_Obsolete>
        <ARI_PrevalenceDesc>Worldwide prevalence: 1.0 cases per 200,000 to 500,000. US prevalence: 1.0 cases per 200,000 to 500,000. Note on the US figure: Searched afterwards. Source: NORD / ACR, rare myositis specific autoantibody phenotype.</ARI_PrevalenceDesc>
        <ARI_SNOMED>838312001</ARI_SNOMED>
        <ARI_UMLS>C5395057</ARI_UMLS>
        <ARI_Version>2.1</ARI_Version>
        <rdfs:comment>Anti-NXP2 dermatomyositis is an autoimmune disorder characterized by autoantibodies targeting nuclear matrix protein 2, affecting both children and adults. It features severe proximal muscle weakness, peripheral edema, and widespread calcium deposits in the skin and muscle tissue (calcinosis), particularly in pediatric patients. Treatment involves sustained immunomodulatory therapy to suppress inflammation and prevent progressive calcinosis.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>Calcinosis due to childhood type dermatomyositis</rdfs:label>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


