        <ARI_PrevalenceDesc>Worldwide prevalence: 1.0 cases per 2,000 to 3,000. US prevalence: 1.0 cases per 1,754 to 10,000.</ARI_PrevalenceDesc>
        <ARI_Pubmed>https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/29129296/</ARI_Pubmed>
        <ARI_RefLink>American Partnership for Eosinophilic Disorders | http://www.apfed.org/</ARI_RefLink>
        <ARI_RefLink>CURED Foundation - Non-Profit for EGID Research | https://curedfoundation.org/</ARI_RefLink>
        <ARI_RefLink>Link to Cleveland Clinic | https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/14321-eosinophilic-esophagitis</ARI_RefLink>
        <ARI_RefLink>Link to Healthline | https://www.healthline.com/health/eosinophilic-esophagitis</ARI_RefLink>
        <ARI_RefLink>Link to Mayo Clinic | https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/eosinophilic-esophagitis/symptoms-causes/syc-20372197</ARI_RefLink>
        <ARI_SNOMED>235599003</ARI_SNOMED>
        <ARI_SurveyCode>EOE</ARI_SurveyCode>
        <ARI_Synonym>Eosinophilic Oesophagitis</ARI_Synonym>
        <ARI_UMLS>C0341106</ARI_UMLS>
        <ARI_Version>2</ARI_Version>
        <rdfs:comment>Eosinophilic esophagitis, EoE, is a buildup of white blood cells (eosinophils) in the throat (esophagus). This causes chronic inflammation and leads to problems swallowing. EoE has only recently been recognized as a disease. The condition is characterized as an immune system reaction, but is more like an allergy than an autoimmune disease.  EoE appears to be more common among those who suffer from allergies and asthma as well as autoimmune conditions such as celiac disease, and mast cell disorders. Mast cells are involved in the body&apos;s allergic response system.  EoE was first described in 1993 with a report of 12 patients who had dysphagia, normal endoscopy, no acid reflux, and intraepithelial eosinophilia. The authors recognized that these patients had a distinct disease. Since then, the disease has increased in prevalence. Evidence for EoE being an autoimmune disease is limited. Symptoms do not respond to gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD) medication.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>Eosinophilic esophagitis</rdfs:label>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


