        <ARI_ClinicalSubtype>Epilepsy, Focal - Seizures originate in one part of the brain.</ARI_ClinicalSubtype>
        <ARI_ClinicalSubtype>Epilepsy, Generalized - Affects both hemispheres of the brain.</ARI_ClinicalSubtype>
        <ARI_DOID>1826</ARI_DOID>
        <ARI_DXCODE>84757009</ARI_DXCODE>
        <ARI_DefSource>Kobau 2023; https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/37031584/</ARI_DefSource>
        <ARI_ICD10>G40.909</ARI_ICD10>
        <ARI_ID>ARI:0001087</ARI_ID>
        <ARI_MESH>D004827</ARI_MESH>
        <ARI_MONDO>0005027</ARI_MONDO>
        <ARI_NCI>C3020</ARI_NCI>
        <ARI_OMOP>380378</ARI_OMOP>
        <ARI_Obsolete>false</ARI_Obsolete>
        <ARI_PrevalenceDesc>Worldwide prevalence: 1.0 cases per 100 to 200. US prevalence: Many cases of epilepsy are not autoimmune.</ARI_PrevalenceDesc>
        <ARI_Pubmed>https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/37031584/</ARI_Pubmed>
        <ARI_RefLink>Link to Cleveland Clinic | https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/17636-epilepsy</ARI_RefLink>
        <ARI_RefLink>Link to Healthline | https://www.healthline.com/health/epilepsy</ARI_RefLink>
        <ARI_RefLink>Link to Mayo Clinic | https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/epilepsy/symptoms-causes/syc-20350093</ARI_RefLink>
        <ARI_RefLink>The Defeating Epilepsy Foundation | https://www.defeatingepilepsy.org/</ARI_RefLink>
        <ARI_RefLink>The Epilepsy Foundation | https://www.epilepsy.com/</ARI_RefLink>
        <ARI_SNOMED>84757009</ARI_SNOMED>
        <ARI_SurveyCode>EP</ARI_SurveyCode>
        <ARI_UMLS>C0014544</ARI_UMLS>
        <ARI_Version>2</ARI_Version>
        <rdfs:comment>Epilepsy is the repeated occurrence of seizures, which are short periods of abnormally excessive or synchronous brain activity in the brain marked by uncontrolled shaking and loss of consciousness. Seizures usually last less than two minutes but it takes time to return to normal. Since a number of autoimmune diseases that affect the brain can lead to seizures, epilepsy has been listed in some lists as an autoimmune disease. However, unless the cause of the seizure is an underlying autoimmune disease, epilepsy itself is not an autoimmune disease.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>Epilepsy</rdfs:label>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


