        <ARI_PrevalenceDesc>Worldwide incidence: 1.0 cases per 2 to 5. US incidence: 1.0 cases per 2 to 5 treated patients. Note on the US figure: Searched afterwards. Source: Martins et al. / Postow et al. (NEJM), incidence of irAEs in patients on ICI therapy.</ARI_PrevalenceDesc>
        <ARI_Version>2.1</ARI_Version>
        <rdfs:comment>Immune checkpoint inhibitor-induced autoimmunity encompasses a diverse range of autoimmune and inflammatory toxicities triggered by cancer immunotherapies targeting CTLA-4, PD-1, or PD-L1. Removing these inhibitory immune checkpoints unleashes autoreactive T cells, resulting in colitis, hepatitis, pneumonitis, endocrinopathies, or dermatitis. Treatment involves pausing immunotherapy and administering systemic corticosteroids or immunosuppressive drugs.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>Immune checkpoint inhibitor-induced autoimmunity</rdfs:label>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


