        <ARI_PrevalenceDesc>Worldwide prevalence: 1.0 cases per 1,000,000; Extremely rare. US prevalence: Extremely rare. Note on the US figure: Searched afterwards. Source: Barzaghi et al. / Orphanet, rare FOXP3 deficiency.</ARI_PrevalenceDesc>
        <ARI_UMLS>C0342288</ARI_UMLS>
        <ARI_Version>2.1</ARI_Version>
        <rdfs:comment>IPEX syndrome (immune dysregulation, polyendocrinopathy, enteropathy, X-linked) is a rare genetic autoimmune disorder caused by mutations in the FOXP3 gene, the master regulator of regulatory T cells. Lacking functional Tregs, infants develop severe intractable secretory diarrhea, early-onset type 1 diabetes, eczema, and widespread autoimmune destruction. Definitive treatment requires hematopoietic stem cell transplantation.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>Immune Dysregulation, Polyendocrinopathy, Enteropathy, X-Linked Syndrome</rdfs:label>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


