        <ARI_NCI>C84786</ARI_NCI>
        <ARI_OMIM>147421</ARI_OMIM>
        <ARI_OMOP>4216406</ARI_OMOP>
        <ARI_ORPHANET>611</ARI_ORPHANET>
        <ARI_Obsolete>false</ARI_Obsolete>
        <ARI_PrevalenceDesc>Worldwide prevalence: 1.0 cases per 20,000 to 50,000. US prevalence: 1.0 cases per 125,000. Note on the US figure: Searched afterwards. Source: Needham et al. / NORD, prevalence approx 1-2 per 100,000. Sheet row 124, a duplicate of row 123, gives US prevalence: 1.0 cases per 100,000.</ARI_PrevalenceDesc>
        <ARI_Pubmed>https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/30837708/</ARI_Pubmed>
        <ARI_RefLink>Getting Up | https://www.gettingup.org/</ARI_RefLink>
        <ARI_RefLink>Link to Cleveland Clinic | https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/15700-inclusion-body-myositis</ARI_RefLink>
        <ARI_RefLink>Link to Healthline | https://www.healthline.com/health/inclusion-body-myositis</ARI_RefLink>
        <ARI_RefLink>Muscular Dystrophy Association – USA National Office | https://www.mda.org/</ARI_RefLink>
        <ARI_RefLink>Myositis Association | https://www.myositis.org/</ARI_RefLink>
        <ARI_RefLink>Myositis Support &amp; Understanding | https://understandingmyositis.org/blog/</ARI_RefLink>
        <ARI_RefLink>The Cure IBM Blog | https://cureibm.org/inclusion-body-myositis-blog/</ARI_RefLink>
        <ARI_SNOMED>72315009</ARI_SNOMED>
        <ARI_SurveyCode>IBM</ARI_SurveyCode>
        <ARI_Synonym>IBM</ARI_Synonym>
        <ARI_Synonym>Sporadic Inclusion Body Myositis</ARI_Synonym>
        <ARI_Synonym>sIBM</ARI_Synonym>
        <ARI_UMLS>C0238190</ARI_UMLS>
        <ARI_Version>2</ARI_Version>
        <rdfs:comment>Inclusion Body Myositis (IBM) is a chronic inflammatory muscle disease that causes painless weakening of the muscle. It is a chronic, slowly progressive condition that can lead to disability over the years. Studies show that IBM is more common in men than in women. Most people need assistance with basic daily activities within 15 years, and some people will need to use a wheelchair. The illness can be difficult to diagnose and is often first mistaken as polymyositis. It is not directly genetic, but predispositions may be present. Lifespan is thought to be normal, but severe complications include aspiration pneumonia that can lead to loss of life. There is currently no cure and standard course of treatment for IBM. Management options include physical therapy, exercise, speech therapy, and fall prevention.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>Inclusion body myositis</rdfs:label>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


