        <ARI_Version>2.1</ARI_Version>
        <rdfs:comment>Autoimmune chylomicronemia is a rare metabolic disorder caused by autoantibodies that bind to GPIHBP1, a protein essential for transporting lipoprotein lipase onto capillary walls. By blocking this interaction, the clearance of triglyceride-rich chylomicrons from the bloodstream is inhibited, causing severe hypertriglyceridemia, recurrent pancreatitis, and eruptive xanthomas. Management combines dietary fat restriction with targeted immunosuppressive therapies.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>Primary acquired chylomicronemia</rdfs:label>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


