        <ARI_ClinicalSubtype>Systemic Mastocytosis, Aggressive - Severe organ damage.</ARI_ClinicalSubtype>
        <ARI_ClinicalSubtype>Systemic Mastocytosis, Indolent - Slow progression, mild symptoms.</ARI_ClinicalSubtype>
        <ARI_DOID>349</ARI_DOID>
        <ARI_DXCODE>397016004</ARI_DXCODE>
        <ARI_DefSource>Jørgensen 2025; https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11799073/</ARI_DefSource>
        <ARI_ICD10>D47.02</ARI_ICD10>
        <ARI_ID>ARI:0001197</ARI_ID>
        <ARI_MESH>D034721</ARI_MESH>
        <ARI_MONDO>0016586</ARI_MONDO>
        <ARI_NCI>C9235</ARI_NCI>
        <ARI_OMOP>4246284</ARI_OMOP>
        <ARI_ORPHANET>2467</ARI_ORPHANET>
        <ARI_Obsolete>false</ARI_Obsolete>
        <ARI_PrevalenceDesc>Worldwide prevalence: 1.0 cases per 7,000 to 10,000. US prevalence: GARD suggests under 200,000 and cites the value for the cutaneous form.</ARI_PrevalenceDesc>
        <ARI_RefLink>Link to Cleveland Clinic | https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/24386-systemic-mastocytosis</ARI_RefLink>
        <ARI_RefLink>Link to Healthline | https://www.healthline.com/health/mast-cell-activation-syndrome</ARI_RefLink>
        <ARI_SNOMED>397016004</ARI_SNOMED>
        <ARI_SurveyCode>SMCD</ARI_SurveyCode>
        <ARI_UMLS>C0221013</ARI_UMLS>
        <ARI_Version>2</ARI_Version>
        <rdfs:comment>Systemic mastocytosis is caused when too many mast cells accumulate in the body. It is not an autoimmune disease, but a disease of the immune system, specifically mast cells. Mast cells transport histamine, which is involved in allergic responses.  

When mastocytosis is limited to the skin, it is called cutaneous mastocytosis, and is a less severe form of the disease.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>Systemic mast cell disease</rdfs:label>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


