        <ARI_ORPHANET>90033</ARI_ORPHANET>
        <ARI_Obsolete>false</ARI_Obsolete>
        <ARI_PrevalenceDesc>Worldwide prevalence: 1.0 cases per 5,000 to 10,000. US prevalence: 1.0 cases per 7,692 to 9,091.</ARI_PrevalenceDesc>
        <ARI_RefLink>Link to Cleveland Clinic | https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/22349-autoimmune-hemolytic-anemia</ARI_RefLink>
        <ARI_RefLink>Warm Autoimmune Hemolytic Anemia | https://www.facebook.com/groups/302296907404576/</ARI_RefLink>
        <ARI_SNOMED>3978000</ARI_SNOMED>
        <ARI_SurveyCode>WAHA</ARI_SurveyCode>
        <ARI_SynonymWithdrawn>Acquired autoimmune hemolytic anemia | broader | acquired AIHA covers warm, cold, mixed and drug-induced types; this entity is the warm type</ARI_SynonymWithdrawn>
        <ARI_SynonymWithdrawn>Idiopathic autoimmune hemolytic anemia | broader | primary AIHA also covers the cold types</ARI_SynonymWithdrawn>
        <ARI_SynonymWithdrawn>Immune hemolytic anemia | broader | includes warm AIHA, cold agglutinin disease, paroxysmal cold haemoglobinuria, drug-induced and alloimmune haemolysis</ARI_SynonymWithdrawn>
        <ARI_SynonymWithdrawn>Immunohemolytic anemia | broader | = immune haemolytic anaemia, the broad category</ARI_SynonymWithdrawn>
        <ARI_UMLS>C0272118</ARI_UMLS>
        <ARI_Version>2</ARI_Version>
        <rdfs:comment>Warm autoimmune hemolytic anemia is caused when a person&apos;s antibodies attack and destroy their red blood cells.  Unlike cold agglutinin disease, the autoimmune destruction of cells occurs when a person&apos;s body temperature is normal or above normal.  It leads to low red blood cell levels in the body.  

Some cases of this disease are caused by medications and are not autoimmune.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>Warm autoimmune hemolytic anemia</rdfs:label>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


