        <ARI_PrevalenceDesc>Worldwide prevalence: 1.0 cases per 100,000 to 200,000. US prevalence: 1.0 cases per 100,000 to 200,000. Note on the US figure: Searched afterwards. Source: Stone et al. / Mayo Clinic, rare non-infectious thoracic aortitis.</ARI_PrevalenceDesc>
        <ARI_Synonym>Autoimmune aortitis</ARI_Synonym>
        <ARI_Synonym>clinically isolated aortitis</ARI_Synonym>
        <ARI_Synonym>idiopathic active aortitis</ARI_Synonym>
        <ARI_Synonym>idiopathic aortitis</ARI_Synonym>
        <ARI_Synonym>inflammatory aortitis without systemic vasculitis</ARI_Synonym>
        <ARI_Synonym>isolated aortitis</ARI_Synonym>
        <ARI_Synonym>isolated ascending aortitis</ARI_Synonym>
        <ARI_Synonym>isolated idiopathic aortitis</ARI_Synonym>
        <ARI_Synonym>isolated noninfectious aortitis</ARI_Synonym>
        <ARI_Synonym>isolated thoracic aortitis</ARI_Synonym>
        <ARI_Synonym>non-infectious ascending aortitis</ARI_Synonym>
        <ARI_Synonym>noninfectious aortitis</ARI_Synonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Autoimmune aortitis is an inflammatory condition of the aorta characterized by immune-mediated infiltration of the arterial wall that occurs in the absence of generalized systemic vasculitis. Chronic inflammation can weaken the structural integrity of the thoracic or abdominal aorta, predisposing patients to aneurysm formation, aortic insufficiency, or dissection. Corticosteroids and immunosuppressive agents are the mainstay of treatment to prevent vascular complications. Imaging shows an ascending aortic aneurysm or aortic root dilation in 70–85% of patients, and 30–40% have no symptoms before dissection or rupture.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>Autoimmune aortitis (isolated, non-vasculitic)</rdfs:label>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


