        <ARI_PrevalenceDesc>Worldwide prevalence: 1.0 cases per 1,000,000. US prevalence: 1.0 cases per 1,000,000. Note on the US figure: Searched afterwards. Source: Akram et al. / Orphanet, rare adult/pediatric intractable enteropathy.</ARI_PrevalenceDesc>
        <rdfs:comment>Autoimmune enteropathy is an extremely rare disease characterized by diarrhea that does not respond to treatment and may require intravenous feeding to sustain the patient. It is diagnosed most frequently in infants and  is increasingly being diagnosed in adults, but the symptoms are the same as many more common diseases, so correct diagnosis is difficult.   It is more frequently diagnosed in patients already diagnosed with other autoimmune diseases such as APECED type 1. Small intestinal biopsy shows total villous atrophy in 90–100% of patients.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>Autoimmune enteropathy</rdfs:label>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


