        <ARI_Synonym>AGID</ARI_Synonym>
        <ARI_Synonym>GAIN</ARI_Synonym>
        <ARI_Synonym>autoimmune GI dysmotility</ARI_Synonym>
        <ARI_Synonym>autoimmune achalasia</ARI_Synonym>
        <ARI_Synonym>autoimmune dysautonomia limited to the gut</ARI_Synonym>
        <ARI_Synonym>autoimmune enteric neuropathy</ARI_Synonym>
        <ARI_Synonym>autoimmune gastrointestinal pseudo-obstruction</ARI_Synonym>
        <ARI_Synonym>gastrointestinal autoimmune dysmotility</ARI_Synonym>
        <ARI_Synonym>gastrointestinal autoimmune neuropathy</ARI_Synonym>
        <ARI_Synonym>gut autoimmune enteric neuropathy</ARI_Synonym>
        <ARI_Synonym>idiopathic achalasia with autoimmune features</ARI_Synonym>
        <ARI_Synonym>paraneoplastic gastrointestinal dysmotility</ARI_Synonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Autoimmune gastrointestinal dysmotility is a condition where autoantibodies or immune cells attack the enteric nervous system, impairing coordinated contractions throughout the digestive tract. Manifestations include severe dysphagia, delayed gastric emptying, pseudo-obstruction, or autoimmune achalasia of the esophagus. Therapy focuses on supportive nutritional interventions, prokinetic agents, and immunosuppressive medications to mitigate enteric nerve damage.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>Autoimmune gastrointestinal dysmotility syndrome (Idiopathic esophageal achalasia with autoimmune features or Autoimmune achalasia)</rdfs:label>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


