        <ARI_Synonym>Autoimmune hemophilia B</ARI_Synonym>
        <ARI_Synonym>acquired F9 deficiency</ARI_Synonym>
        <ARI_Synonym>acquired FIX deficiency</ARI_Synonym>
        <ARI_Synonym>acquired anti-FIX hemophilia</ARI_Synonym>
        <ARI_Synonym>acquired coagulation factor IX inhibitor</ARI_Synonym>
        <ARI_Synonym>acquired factor IX deficiency</ARI_Synonym>
        <ARI_Synonym>acquired haemophilia B</ARI_Synonym>
        <ARI_Synonym>acquired hemophilia B</ARI_Synonym>
        <ARI_Synonym>acquired hemophilia due to factor IX inhibitor</ARI_Synonym>
        <ARI_Synonym>anti-factor IX antibody bleeding disorder</ARI_Synonym>
        <ARI_Synonym>autoimmune factor IX deficiency</ARI_Synonym>
        <ARI_Synonym>factor IX inhibitor</ARI_Synonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Autoimmune hemophilia B is an acquired bleeding disorder where the immune system generates neutralizing autoantibodies against coagulation factor IX. The inactivation of factor IX disrupts the clotting cascade, resulting in spontaneous, severe hematomas and deep tissue hemorrhage in individuals without a personal history of bleeding disorders. Management involves bypassing agents to stop acute hemorrhage and immunosuppressive therapy to eliminate the inhibitor.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>Autoimmune hemophilia B (anti-factor IX antibody)</rdfs:label>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


