        <ARI_Synonym>Autoimmune nodopathy</ARI_Synonym>
        <ARI_Synonym>IgG4 nodopathy</ARI_Synonym>
        <ARI_Synonym>anti-CASPR1 neuropathy</ARI_Synonym>
        <ARI_Synonym>anti-CNTN1 nodopathy</ARI_Synonym>
        <ARI_Synonym>anti-Caspr1 nodopathy</ARI_Synonym>
        <ARI_Synonym>anti-NF155 nodopathy</ARI_Synonym>
        <ARI_Synonym>anti-NF186 nodopathy</ARI_Synonym>
        <ARI_Synonym>anti-contactin-1 neuropathy</ARI_Synonym>
        <ARI_Synonym>anti-neurofascin-155 neuropathy</ARI_Synonym>
        <ARI_Synonym>antibody-mediated nodo-paranodopathy</ARI_Synonym>
        <ARI_Synonym>autoimmune nodo-paranodopathy</ARI_Synonym>
        <ARI_Synonym>autoimmune nodopathies</ARI_Synonym>
        <ARI_Synonym>autoimmune peripheral neuropathy with nodal/paranodal antibodies</ARI_Synonym>
        <ARI_Synonym>nodo-paranodopathy</ARI_Synonym>
        <ARI_Synonym>nodopathy</ARI_Synonym>
        <ARI_Synonym>pan-neurofascin nodopathy</ARI_Synonym>
        <ARI_Synonym>paranodopathy</ARI_Synonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Autoimmune nodopathies are a group of antibody-mediated peripheral neuropathies where autoantibodies, commonly IgG4, target cell adhesion molecules like neurofascin-155, contactin-1, or Caspr1 at the nodes of Ranvier. This disrupts electrical signal transmission along nerve fibers, causing severe muscle weakness, sensory ataxia, and prominent tremors. Unlike classic demyelinating polyneuropathy, these disorders often respond better to B-cell depleting therapies like rituximab than IVIg. 80–90% of patients are refractory to IVIg, with a high response rate to rituximab.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>Autoimmune nodopathies (anti-NF155, anti-CNTN1, anti-Caspr1, etc.)</rdfs:label>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


