        <ARI_PrevalenceDesc>Worldwide prevalence: 1.0 cases per 100 to 500. US prevalence: 1.0 cases per 100 to 500. Note on the US figure: Searched afterwards. Source: Restrepo et al. / ASRM, detected in 2-5% of couples evaluated for infertility.</ARI_PrevalenceDesc>
        <ARI_Synonym>Autoimmunity against spermatozoa</ARI_Synonym>
        <ARI_Synonym>Immunoinfertility</ARI_Synonym>
        <ARI_Synonym>Immunological infertility</ARI_Synonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Infertility associated with antisperm antibodies occurs when the immune system generates autoantibodies that target sperm antigens in either men or women. These antibodies coat sperm cells, impairing motility, causing agglutination, or blocking sperm-egg binding and fertilization. Treatment involves assisted reproductive technologies, such as intrauterine insemination or in vitro fertilization with intracytoplasmic sperm injection. Laboratory findings include failure of cervical mucus penetration on postcoital testing (70–85%), spontaneous macroscopic or microscopic sperm agglutination in the ejaculate (70–80%), and markedly decreased progressive sperm forward motility (65–75%).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>Infertility associated with Antisperm Antibodies</rdfs:label>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


